IMPORT prelude

-- Liquor Control Act 1988 (WA) -- may I buy and drink alcohol now, and what can happen to me if I do?
--
-- SEAT: the young person -- someone who is, or may be, a "juvenile" (under 18) -- asking "May I buy and drink alcohol
-- now?". Every decision is phrased as that person asks it, from facts they could state. Where Division 9 speaks to a
-- licensee, a supplier or an authorised person, the encoding says what it means for the young person: whether the
-- person who gave them a drink broke the law, whether they can be made to show ID or leave, and what can be taken.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3(1) ("juvenile"); Part 4 Division 9: s 120 (when juveniles may be on licensed premises, the
-- limbs (1)(b), (1)(f) and (2)), s 121(1) (sale or supply to a juvenile on licensed premises: the seller's fine), s 121(3)
-- (getting someone to buy for me), s 121(4), (5) (being on licensed premises), s 121(6)-(8) (out-of-bounds areas), s 121(7a)
-- (loitering after being made to leave), s 121(10), (11) (being employed to serve liquor), s 122(1), (2), (3), (4)
-- (regulated premises; a road counts for sale, supply, purchase and obtaining), s 122A (supply on unlicensed premises,
-- including by a parent at home), s 123 (buying, bringing, drinking, possessing), s 124 (being sent to get liquor), s 125(1)(b)
-- (the seller's belief that I was 18) and s 125(2)(b) ("responsible adult"), s 126 (proof of age, confiscation, removal,
-- re-entry); s 167(3), (4) (the modified penalty on an infringement notice, and the 28 days to pay).
--
-- Edition: the WA Legislation consolidation as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49727.txt (retrieved
-- 2026-09-07), whose compilation table runs to the Liquor Control Amendment Act 2025 (No. 20 of 2025), Pt. 3 in operation
-- 1 Jul 2026. No regulations are used: the Liquor Control Regulations 1989 are not deposited, so the forms of evidence they
-- prescribe (s 126(1)(b)(i)(IV)), the circumstances they prescribe (s 122A(3)(e)) and the offences for which an
-- infringement notice may be given (s 167(2)(a)) are INPUTS or are left to the reader, as said where they arise.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: licensing (Parts 3, 4 Divisions 1-8A), the general offences that fall on everyone whatever their age
-- (s 119: drinking in a place without the occupier's consent, at a sports ground, on a road), drunkenness and the duties of
-- licensees (s 115), banned drinkers and barring (Part 5A and following), s 120(1)(a), (c), (d), (e) (clubs, receptions,
-- transport facilities, areas approved by the Director -- the accompanied and meal limbs of (c) are covered by s 121(5)(a)),
-- s 121(9) (the adult who leaves me behind), s 125(1)(a), (c) (the licensee's other defences), approvals under ss 126A,
-- 126B beyond their effect, and the fines in force before the edition deposited (the penalty figures here are the ones in
-- the deposited text, which No. 20 of 2025 amended; earlier figures were not read).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the design defaults ruled on
-- 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice.
-- Dates are numbers written YYYYMMDD. Judgments the Act hands to others (the authorised person's suspicion, the Director's
-- approval) are INPUTS.

§ `Liquor Control Act 1988 -- juveniles`

-- Where I am. "licensed premises" (s 3(1)): the premises specified or defined by the Director for a licence or permit.
-- "regulated premises" (ss 3(1), 122(1)): premises that are not licensed but are, for example, an area next to and used
-- with licensed premises, a place a permit covers, a hall or venue open to the public with amusements or refreshments at a
-- charge, a cafe or other place serving food or soft drinks, or an unlicensed club. Which of these a place is, is asked
-- as a fact. A road (with its footpath, and a vehicle on it) is regulated premises "for the purpose only of" the sale or
-- supply to, or purchase or obtaining by, a juvenile (s 122(1)(g)), so it is its own choice here.
DECLARE Place IS ONE OF
    `licensed premises`
    `regulated premises`
    `a road or footpath`
    `another place the public can go`
    `a private home or other private place`

-- Who is with me. s 125(2)(b): "a person shall be taken to be a responsible adult if that person was an adult who is a
-- parent, stepparent, spouse, de facto partner or legal guardian of the juvenile, or other person in loco parentis".
DECLARE Companion IS ONE OF
    `my parent`
    `my step-parent`
    `my spouse or de facto partner`
    `my legal guardian`
    `another adult standing in place of my parent`
    `another adult or friend`
    `nobody`

-- Why I am there: the exceptions of ss 120 and 121(5) other than being accompanied.
DECLARE Reason IS ONE OF
    `no particular reason`
    `to get a meal`
    `approved juvenile entertainment where no liquor is sold, supplied or consumed`
    `an approved training course`
    `I work there, not selling or serving liquor`
    `I live there as family of the licensee, the manager or an employee`
    `I am carrying on a lawful business there and not drinking`
    `I am only passing through, with no other convenient way`

-- Who gave me the drink, for s 122A.
DECLARE Supplier IS ONE OF
    `my parent or guardian`
    `another person, with my parent's or guardian's consent`
    `another person, without that consent`

-- Who sold or supplied it, for the fine they face.
DECLARE `Seller role` IS ONE OF
    `the licensee or a manager`
    `anyone else who sold or supplied it`

-- What I showed when asked my age (s 126(1)(b)(i), (2C)).
DECLARE Evidence IS ONE OF
    `a current Australian driver's licence with a photograph`
    `a current passport`
    `a current WA photo card`
    `another form of evidence prescribed by the regulations`
    `evidence issued in digital form, shown in that form or by its QR code`
    `a photograph on my phone of a physical card`
    `an expired passport`
    `a student card`
    `nothing`

-- The offences that fall on the juvenile.
DECLARE `My offence` IS ONE OF
    `s 121(3) getting someone to buy liquor for me on licensed premises`
    `s 121(4) being on licensed premises`
    `s 121(7) not leaving an out-of-bounds area`
    `s 121(7a) staying next to the premises after being made to leave`
    `s 122(3) being on regulated premises`
    `s 123(1) buying, bringing or drinking liquor on licensed or regulated premises`
    `s 123(2) having liquor in a public place`
    `s 126(2) not complying with, or lying to, a request for my age`
    `s 126(4) going back in within 24 hours`
    `s 126(5) staying next to the premises after being made to leave`

§§ `3(1) -- am I a juvenile`

-- s 3(1): "juvenile means a person under the age of 18 years". With dates written YYYYMMDD, my 18th birthday is my date of
-- birth plus 180000. (INFERENCE: for a 29 February birthday this puts the 18th birthday on 1 March of a common year; the
-- Act does not say.)
@export Am I a juvenile -- under 18 -- for the Liquor Control Act (s 3(1))?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc Your date of birth, written YYYYMMDD
      `the date` IS A NUMBER @desc The date in question, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a juvenile` IF
    `the date` < (`my date of birth` PLUS 180000)

§§ `125(2)(b) -- a responsible adult`

GIVEN `the companion` IS A Companion
      `the companion is an adult` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a responsible adult` IF
    `the companion is an adult` AND `is a family member or guardian` `the companion`
    WHERE
        `is a family member or guardian` c MEANS
            CONSIDER c
            WHEN `my parent` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `my step-parent` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `my spouse or de facto partner` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `my legal guardian` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `another adult standing in place of my parent` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `120, 121(4), (5) -- may I be on licensed premises`

-- s 121(4): "where a juvenile enters or remains on any part of the licensed premises -- ... (c) the juvenile, ... each commit
-- an offence." s 121(5): not (a) a juvenile "(i) accompanied by, and under the supervision of, a responsible adult; or (ii) on
-- the premises for the purpose of obtaining a meal"; (b) approved juvenile entertainment while "liquor is not sold, supplied
-- or consumed there"; (c) an approved training course; (d) "a juvenile employed on the premises otherwise than in the sale or
-- supply of liquor"; (e) "if section 120 applies". s 120(1)(b): family of the licensee, occupier, manager or an employee who
-- resides there; (1)(f): "present only for the purpose of carrying on a lawful business, and does not consume liquor";
-- (2): "solely for the purpose of passing to or from" another part, with "no other convenient means of passage".
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a section 121(5) or 120 reason` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `no particular reason` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is it an offence for me to be on these licensed premises (s 121(4), (5))?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason @desc Why you are there
      `who is with me` IS A Companion @desc Who is with you
      `they are an adult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person with you is an adult (18 or over)
      `I am under their supervision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person is supervising you while you are there
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence for me to be on licensed premises` IF
        (NOT (`is a responsible adult` `who is with me` `they are an adult` AND `I am under their supervision`))
    AND (NOT (`is a section 121(5) or 120 reason` `the reason`))

§§ `121(6)-(8) -- out-of-bounds areas`

-- s 121(6): a licensee may, or if the Director requires shall, declare the premises or part "out of bounds to juveniles".
-- s 121(7): where a juvenile enters such an area "an authorised person may require the juvenile to leave ... and, if the
-- juvenile fails to do so -- (a) the juvenile commits an offence". s 121(8): (6) and (7) do not apply to a juvenile within
-- s 120(1)(b) or (f), 120(2) or 121(5)(c) or (d). Being with a parent, or there for a meal, is NOT among them.
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a section 121(8) reason` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `an approved training course` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I work there, not selling or serving liquor` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I live there as family of the licensee, the manager or an employee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I am carrying on a lawful business there and not drinking` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I am only passing through, with no other convenient way` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Can I be made to leave this out-of-bounds area of licensed premises (s 121(6)-(8))?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason @desc Why you are there
      `the area is declared out of bounds to juveniles now` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether notices at the entrance declare the area out of bounds to juveniles at this time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be made to leave an out-of-bounds area` IF
    `the area is declared out of bounds to juveniles now` AND (NOT (`is a section 121(8) reason` `the reason`))

@export Have I committed an offence by not leaving the out-of-bounds area (s 121(7))?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason @desc Why you are there
      `the area is declared out of bounds to juveniles now` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether notices at the entrance declare the area out of bounds to juveniles at this time
      `an authorised person required me to leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an authorised person told you to leave
      `I left` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you left when told
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed an offence by not leaving` IF
        `Can I be made to leave an out-of-bounds area` `the reason` `the area is declared out of bounds to juveniles now`
    AND `an authorised person required me to leave`
    AND (NOT `I left`)

§§ `122(3), (4) -- may I be on regulated premises`

-- s 122(3): unless the juvenile is the employee or agent of the licensee or occupier, or of a contractor, a juvenile who
-- "enters or remains in any part of regulated premises where liquor is sold or supplied, except for the purpose of obtaining
-- a meal" commits an offence. s 122(4): not a juvenile within s 120(1)(b) or (f), 120(2), or 121(5)(c) or (d). Unlike
-- s 121(5)(a)(i), being accompanied by a responsible adult is NOT an exception here.
@export Is it an offence for me to be in this part of regulated premises (s 122(3), (4))?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason @desc Why you are there
      `liquor is sold or supplied in that part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether liquor is sold or supplied in the part of the premises where you are
      `I work for the licensee, occupier or a contractor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee or agent of the licensee or occupier, or of someone working under a contract with them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence for me to be on regulated premises` IF
        `liquor is sold or supplied in that part`
    AND (NOT `I work for the licensee, occupier or a contractor`)
    AND (NOT `for a meal` `the reason`)
    AND (NOT (`is a section 121(8) reason` `the reason`))
    WHERE
        `for a meal` r MEANS
            CONSIDER r
            WHEN `to get a meal` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `121(3), 122(1)(g), 123 -- buying, carrying and drinking`

-- s 123(1): a juvenile who "(a) purchases or obtains, or attempts to purchase or obtain, liquor from any other person on
-- licensed or regulated premises; or (b) brings liquor on to licensed or regulated premises; or (c) consumes liquor on
-- licensed or regulated premises" commits an offence. s 123(2): a juvenile "who has any liquor in his or her possession or
-- control in any place or on any premises to which the public is permitted to have access" commits an offence.
-- s 122(1)(g): a road is regulated premises for the purchase or obtaining by a juvenile, but not for (b) or (c); there
-- s 123(2) catches what I carry or drink.
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is licensed or regulated for everything` IF
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `licensed premises` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `regulated premises` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the place` IS A Place
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a place the public can go` IF
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `a private home or other private place` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is it an offence for me, as a juvenile, to buy or get liquor here, or try to (s 123(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence for me to buy or get liquor here` IF
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `a road or footpath` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place`

@export Is it an offence for me, as a juvenile, to have liquor with me here (s 123(1)(b), (2))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence for me to have liquor here` IF
    `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place` OR `is a place the public can go` `the place`

@export Is it an offence for me, as a juvenile, to drink liquor here (s 123(1)(c), (2))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence for me to drink here` IF
    `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place` OR `is a place the public can go` `the place`

-- s 121(3): "Where a person, acting at the request of a juvenile on licensed premises, purchases or obtains liquor on behalf
-- of the juvenile on licensed premises, that person and the juvenile each commit an offence."
@export Have I committed an offence by getting someone to buy liquor for me on licensed premises (s 121(3))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you were
      `they bought or got it for me at my request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person bought or got liquor for you because you asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed an offence by getting someone to buy for me` IF
    `is licensed premises` `the place` AND `they bought or got it for me at my request`
    WHERE
        `is licensed premises` p MEANS
            CONSIDER p
            WHEN `licensed premises` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The seat's own question, put together: under 18, is getting or drinking alcohol here an offence of mine under Division 9?
-- From 18 none of these offences can touch me (s 119 and the other general offences still can; not encoded).
@export May I buy and drink alcohol here now without committing a juvenile offence (ss 3(1), 123)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc Your date of birth, written YYYYMMDD
      `the date` IS A NUMBER @desc The date in question, written YYYYMMDD
      `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I buy and drink alcohol here now` IF
        (NOT `Am I a juvenile` `my date of birth` `the date`)
    OR (    (NOT `Is it an offence for me to buy or get liquor here` `the place`)
        AND (NOT `Is it an offence for me to drink here` `the place`))

§§ `121(1), 122(2), 122A, 124, 125(1)(b) -- the person who gives me alcohol`

-- s 122A(2): "A person must not supply liquor to a juvenile unless that person is on unlicensed premises and the person --
-- (a) is the parent or guardian of the juvenile; or (b) subject to subsection (3), has obtained the consent of the parent or
-- guardian". s 122A(3): with consent, not "(a) if, at the time that the parent or guardian ... gives consent, the parent or
-- guardian is drunk; or (b) if the person is drunk; or (c) if the juvenile is drunk; or (d) if the person is unable to
-- supervise the consumption ...; or (e) in circumstances prescribed by the regulations" (an INPUT; the regulations are not
-- deposited). s 122A(1): "drunk" here is s 3A(1)(b) and (c) only -- noticeably impaired speech, balance, coordination or
-- behaviour, reasonably believed to come from liquor -- without s 3A(1)(a)'s "on licensed premises or regulated premises".
-- "unlicensed premises" is not defined; INFERENCE: any place that is not licensed premises. But supply on regulated premises,
-- and on a road (s 122(1)(g)), is forbidden to anyone by s 122(2), and on licensed premises by s 121(1); so a lawful supply
-- under s 122A is one at a home or another public place.
@export Is it lawful for this person to give me alcohol here (ss 121(1), 122(2), 122A)?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
      `the supplier` IS A Supplier @desc Who is giving you the alcohol
      `my parent or guardian was drunk when consenting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your parent or guardian was drunk when they gave consent
      `the supplier is drunk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person giving it to you is drunk
      `I am drunk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are drunk
      `the supplier is able to supervise my drinking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person giving it to you can supervise you drinking it
      `a circumstance prescribed by the regulations applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a circumstance prescribed by the Liquor Control Regulations applies
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it lawful for this person to give me alcohol here` IF
        (NOT `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place`)
    AND (NOT `is a road` `the place`)
    AND `the supplier qualifies`
    WHERE
        `is a road` p MEANS
            CONSIDER p
            WHEN `a road or footpath` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `the supplier qualifies` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the supplier`
            WHEN `my parent or guardian` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `another person, with my parent's or guardian's consent` THEN
                    (NOT `my parent or guardian was drunk when consenting`)
                AND (NOT `the supplier is drunk`)
                AND (NOT `I am drunk`)
                AND `the supplier is able to supervise my drinking`
                AND (NOT `a circumstance prescribed by the regulations applies`)
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The fine on the person who gave it, where the supply is an offence. s 121(1) penalty: "(a) for the licensee or a manager, a
-- fine of $15 000; (b) for the sale or supply by any other person, a fine of $6 000" ((c) $3 000 for one who only permits it,
-- not encoded). s 122(2): "a fine of $15 000". s 122A(2), (3): "a fine of $10 000".
@export If giving me alcohol was an offence, what is the most the person who gave it can be fined, in dollars?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you were given it
      `the role` IS A `Seller role` @desc Who sold or supplied it
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the supplier can be fined` IS
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `licensed premises` THEN
        CONSIDER `the role`
        WHEN `the licensee or a manager` THEN 15000
        OTHERWISE 6000
    WHEN `regulated premises` THEN 15000
    WHEN `a road or footpath` THEN 15000
    OTHERWISE 10000

-- s 125(1)(b): a defence for the person who sold or supplied the liquor to show "(i) the person believed on reasonable grounds
-- that the alleged juvenile was not a juvenile; and (ii) that the juvenile was of or above the age of 16 years".
@export Could the person who sold me liquor rely on believing I was 18 (s 125(1)(b))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years when you were sold it
      `they believed on reasonable grounds I was 18 or over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the seller believed, on reasonable grounds, that you were 18 or over
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could the seller rely on believing I was 18` IF
    `my age` AT LEAST 16 AND `they believed on reasonable grounds I was 18 or over`

-- s 124: "Where a person sends another person, knowing or believing that other person to be a juvenile, to any licensed
-- premises or regulated premises for the purpose of obtaining liquor, the person commits an offence." Penalty $3 000.
@export Did the person who sent me to get liquor commit an offence (s 124)?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you were sent
      `I was sent to get liquor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were sent there to get liquor
      `they knew or believed I was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who sent you knew or believed you were under 18
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Did the person who sent me commit an offence` IF
    `I was sent to get liquor` AND `they knew or believed I was under 18` AND `sent to licensed or regulated premises`
    WHERE
        `sent to licensed or regulated premises` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the place`
            WHEN `a road or footpath` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place`

§§ `121(10), (11) -- may I be employed to serve liquor`

-- s 121(10): employing or engaging "a juvenile in the sale, supply or serving of liquor" is an offence ($15 000, on the
-- employer). s 121(11): not for serving "liquor ancillary to a meal if -- (a) the juvenile is of or above the age of 16
-- years; and (b) the juvenile's employment or engagement is approved by the Director; and (c) the work ... is supervised at
-- all times; and (d) either -- (i) the work ... will be assessed for ... a prescribed training course ...; or (ii) the
-- juvenile has successfully completed a prescribed training course".
@export May I be employed to sell, supply or serve liquor (s 121(10), (11))?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `only serving liquor with meals` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would only serve liquor ancillary to a meal
      `the Director has approved my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director of Liquor Licensing has approved your employment
      `I am supervised at all times` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your work is supervised at all times
      `the work is assessed for, or I have completed, a prescribed training course` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work will be assessed for a prescribed training course you are doing, or you have completed one that assessed it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I be employed to serve liquor` IF
        `my age` AT LEAST 18
    OR (    `my age` AT LEAST 16
        AND `only serving liquor with meals`
        AND `the Director has approved my employment`
        AND `I am supervised at all times`
        AND `the work is assessed for, or I have completed, a prescribed training course`)

§§ `126 -- proof of age`

-- s 126(1): where an authorised person "knows, or suspects on reasonable grounds, that a person on licensed premises or
-- regulated premises is a juvenile", they "(a) may require the juvenile or suspected juvenile to state his or her age; and
-- (b) if the age stated is false, or appears to be false -- (i) may require ... to produce as evidence of that age" one of
-- the listed documents, "and (ii) if the juvenile does not do so, or the evidence produced does not prove that the person is
-- not a juvenile, may require the juvenile ... to leave the premises". The authorised person's suspicion is an INPUT.
@export Can I be required to state my age (s 126(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
      `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorised person knows, or suspects on reasonable grounds, that you are under 18
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be required to state my age` IF
    `is licensed or regulated for everything` `the place` AND `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18`

@export Can I be required to show evidence of my age (s 126(1)(b)(i))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
      `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorised person knows, or suspects on reasonable grounds, that you are under 18
      `the age I stated is or appears false` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the age you stated is false, or appears to be false
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be required to show evidence of my age` IF
    `Can I be required to state my age` `the place` `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18`
    AND `the age I stated is or appears false`

-- s 126(1)(b)(i): "(I) a current Australian driver's licence with a photograph; or (II) a current passport; or (III) a photo
-- card, as defined in the Western Australian Photo Card Act 2014 section 3, that is current; or (IV) another form of evidence
-- prescribed by the regulations". s 126(2C): digital evidence counts if "originally issued in digital form" and shown "in its
-- original digital form (and not merely a digital reproduction of evidence originally issued in a physical form, such as a
-- digital photograph of a physical card)" or by a QR code.
@export Does what I showed count as evidence of my age (s 126(1)(b)(i), (2C))?
GIVEN `what I showed` IS AN Evidence @desc What you showed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this count as evidence of my age` IF
    CONSIDER `what I showed`
    WHEN `a current Australian driver's licence with a photograph` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a current passport` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a current WA photo card` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another form of evidence prescribed by the regulations` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `evidence issued in digital form, shown in that form or by its QR code` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Can I be made to leave because I did not prove my age (s 126(1)(b)(ii))?
GIVEN `the place` IS A Place @desc Where you are
      `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorised person knows, or suspects on reasonable grounds, that you are under 18
      `the age I stated is or appears false` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the age you stated is false, or appears to be false
      `what I showed` IS AN Evidence @desc What you showed
      `it proves I am 18 or over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you showed proves you are 18 or over
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be made to leave for not proving my age` IF
    `Can I be required to show evidence of my age` `the place` `the authorised person knows or reasonably suspects I am under 18` `the age I stated is or appears false`
    AND (   (NOT `Does this count as evidence of my age` `what I showed`)
         OR (NOT `it proves I am 18 or over`))

-- s 126(2a): if an authorised person "suspects on reasonable grounds that a document produced ... (other than subsection
-- (1)(b)(i)(II)) is a forged, false or counterfeit document, the authorised person may confiscate the document". (II) is a
-- passport. s 126(2D): not evidence produced in digital form; s 126(2E): I need not hand over my phone.
@export Can my ID be confiscated (s 126(2a), (2D))?
GIVEN `what I showed` IS AN Evidence @desc What you showed
      `the authorised person reasonably suspects it is forged, false or counterfeit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorised person suspects, on reasonable grounds, that it is forged, false or counterfeit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my ID be confiscated` IF
    `the authorised person reasonably suspects it is forged, false or counterfeit` AND `is confiscable` `what I showed`
    WHERE
        `is confiscable` e MEANS
            CONSIDER e
            WHEN `a current passport` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `an expired passport` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `evidence issued in digital form, shown in that form or by its QR code` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `nothing` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 126(2): "A person who -- (a) fails, without reasonable excuse, to comply with a requirement under subsection (1); or
-- (b) makes a statement, or produces alleged evidence, that is false or misleading in any material respect in response to
-- the requirement, commits an offence." Penalty $3 000.
@export Have I committed an offence in answering a request for my age (s 126(2))?
GIVEN `I failed to comply with the requirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not do what you were required to do (state your age, show evidence, or leave)
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying
      `what I said or showed was false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you said or showed was false or misleading in a material respect (for example a fake ID)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed an offence answering the request for my age` IF
        (`I failed to comply with the requirement` AND (NOT `I had a reasonable excuse`))
    OR `what I said or showed was false or misleading`

-- s 126(4): a person "who reenters licensed premises or regulated premises within 24 hours of being required to leave, or
-- being removed from, those premises under this section ... commits an offence". INFERENCE: re-entry at exactly 24 hours is
-- within them.
@export Have I committed an offence by going back in (s 126(4))?
GIVEN `I went back in` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you went back into the premises
      `hours since I was made to leave` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after being required to leave, or removed, you went back in
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed an offence by going back in` IF
    `I went back in` AND `hours since I was made to leave` AT MOST 24

§§ `Penalties and infringement notices`

-- The maximum fine for each offence that falls on me, as printed in the deposited text.
@export What is the most I can be fined for this offence, in dollars?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `My offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `s 121(3) getting someone to buy liquor for me on licensed premises` THEN 2000
    WHEN `s 121(4) being on licensed premises` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 121(7) not leaving an out-of-bounds area` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 121(7a) staying next to the premises after being made to leave` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 122(3) being on regulated premises` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 123(1) buying, bringing or drinking liquor on licensed or regulated premises` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 123(2) having liquor in a public place` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 126(2) not complying with, or lying to, a request for my age` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 126(4) going back in within 24 hours` THEN 3000
    WHEN `s 126(5) staying next to the premises after being made to leave` THEN 3000

-- s 167(3): "The modified penalty specified in an infringement notice shall be 10% of the maximum fine for that offence".
-- s 167(2)(a): a notice may be given only for "a prescribed offence"; which offences are prescribed is in the regulations,
-- which are not deposited, so this answers what the notice would ask IF one is given.
@export If I am given an infringement notice for this offence, how much does it ask me to pay, in dollars (s 167(3))?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `My offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What would an infringement notice ask me to pay` IS
    (`What is the most I can be fined` `the offence` TIMES 10) DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 167(2A)(b), (4): I may pay "within a period of 28 days after the giving of the notice"; if I do not, I am "deemed to have
-- so declined" (unless the Director allows more time), and may be prosecuted.
@export How many days do I have to pay an infringement notice (s 167(4))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days do I have to pay an infringement notice` IS 28
